m","idp_source":"gtwo_kml_202611192327","idp_filedate":1795130826000,"nhcTitle":"Offshore of the Southeast U.S. Coast","discussion":"Disorganized showers and thunderstorms extending across portions of  the Florida peninsula and the Bahamas are associated with a frontal  boundary. A non-tropical area of low pressure is forecast to form  along this boundary just east of the Florida peninsula, or over the  northern Bahamas, on Friday. This system could acquire subtropical  or tropical characteristics while it moves generally northward to  north-northeastward near or just offshore of the southeastern United  States coast through the weekend.  1. Regardless of tropical or subtropical development, the system is  expected to produce strong, gusty winds, dangerous surf, rip  currents and coastal flooding along portions of the southeast and  mid-Atlantic U.S. coasts into early next week. In addition, heavy  rainfall and the threat of flash flooding will continue across  portions of the Florida peninsula through Friday, potentially  spreading northward toward coastal portions of the Carolinas and the  mid-Atlantic over the weekend. Interests along the eastern coast of  the United States should continue to monitor the progress of this  system. Additional information on this system, including Gale  Warnings, can be found in High Seas Forecasts issued by the National  Weather Service, as well as products issued by your local National  Weather Service forecast office. \n Formation chance through 48 hours...low...30 percent. \n Formation chance through 7 days...medium...60 percent.","disturbance":1}}]}